Title: The Glass Harbour
Credit: Written by
Author: Manny
Draft date: 08/27/2026

INT. THE GLASS HARBOUR DINER - LATE AFTERNOON #1#

One shot. No cuts. Five minutes, one breath.
A 1956 diner going quietly to seed. Checkerboard tile, chrome stools, boomerang formica. On the plate glass, hand-painted and flaking, a harbour scene and the name of the place, reading backwards from in here. Late gold light comes through it low and cuts the room in half.
We begin eight inches off the floor, gliding forward on a slow dolly. Past the kick-plates of the empty stools. Past a dropped sugar packet. Past scuffed work boots and, across the aisle, white sneakers that have not moved in some time. The camera rises as it travels, comes up over the lip of a corner booth, and finds them.
RAY MERCER, fifty-seven, windbreaker he has owned too long, hands too big for his cup. He got here twenty minutes early.
Across from him, JUNE MERCER, twenty-three. Coat on. Bag still on her shoulder. Sitting on the edge of the bench the way you sit in a waiting room.
Between them: two coffees, a salt shaker, a pepper shaker. Nothing touched.

RAY
You look like her.

JUNE
People say that.

RAY
It's not a bad thing.

JUNE
I didn't say it was.

The camera eases toward Ray. Favoring him. Waiting with him. He moves uncomfortably in his seat.

RAY
Coffee's still bad here. Some things they never fix.

JUNE
You picked this place.

RAY
I thought you'd know it.

JUNE
I know it. It's the same booth.

RAY
They've only got four.

JUNE
Dad.

The word lands on the table like a set of keys. Ray stops.

JUNE (CONT'D)
You called me at work.

RAY
I didn't have your other number.

JUNE
You had it for a year and a half. It's the same one.

He turns his cup a quarter turn. The camera drifts with his hands and finds her over his shoulder.

JUNE (CONT'D)
So why now.

RAY
Does it have to be a why.

JUNE
It's been nine years. Yeah. It has to be a why.

Ray looks at the painted glass.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Is she in the car? Because I'm not doing that today.

RAY
Elaine left in March.

June laughs. It comes out wrong, and once it's out she can't put it back.

JUNE
March.

RAY
June.

JUNE
Five months. You gave it five months and then you thought, you know who I haven't ruined an afternoon with.

RAY
That isn't what this is.

JUNE
It's exactly what this is.

She pauses. Looks at the shaker.
He shoves the salt shaker two inches towards her. The camera goes with it, crossing the table, and comes up on her side. We are with her now.

JUNE (CONT'D)
You sat me down in this booth and you bought me a strawberry milkshake and you told me here. Then you drove me home and by Thursday you were gone.

RAY
I know what I did.

JUNE
You took the good car. You took the Buick because it had the AC and you left mom the one that didn't start in the cold. And then it got cold.

RAY
That car was in my name.

JUNE
I found her on the kitchen floor. She wasn't crying — that's the part nobody — she was sitting with her back against the dishwasher and she wouldn't get up, and I was fourteen, and I made us eggs. At nine at night. It was the only thing I knew how to make.

Ray's jaw works. He says nothing. The camera holds on him, unkind about it.

JUNE (CONT'D)
Then a check every month. No note. Nine years of a man who could find the address but not a pen.

RAY
I sent notes.

JUNE
You didn't send notes.

RAY
Not all of them. I sent some.

JUNE
I would have kept them.

RAY
(too fast, too loud)
I was thirty-four and I was drowning in that house, June, and nobody in it ever once asked me if I —

He hears it. Stops. The room hears it too.
The camera pushes in. Closer than either would allow if they noticed.

JUNE
Say the rest.

RAY
No.

JUNE
If anybody was drowning in that house — say the rest.

RAY
There isn't a rest that helps me.

JUNE
Try anyway.

Ray sets his hands flat on the table. It costs him.

RAY
I knew.

JUNE
What.

RAY
That's what I came to say and I've been sitting here forty minutes not saying it. I knew what I was doing. All that year. It wasn't a fog. I used to sit in the driveway with the engine off and count the windows with a light on. And then I'd go anyway.

Silence. A radio behind the counter goes somewhere sad and finishes.

RAY (CONT'D)
I chose it. Every day I chose it again. Nobody made me. And I picked this room to tell you in because I thought it'd be easier with people around.

June has gone very still.

JUNE
That's worse.

RAY
I know.

JUNE
Do you understand that's worse? I've had nine years to build a man who didn't know what he was doing. I had him all ready. He was weak and he was sad and I could forgive him in about four minutes.

RAY
I'm not asking you to forgive anything.

JUNE
Then what am I supposed to do with you?

Her voice cracks and she's furious that it did. The camera stays close and does not look away.

JUNE (CONT'D)
I don't even know what I'm feeling. I practiced this in the car.

RAY
What'd you practice.

JUNE
Something meaner.

RAY
You can still do it.

JUNE
I know I can.
(beat)
I don't want to.

Ray takes an envelope from the windbreaker. Soft at the corners, yellow along one edge. He slides it to the middle.
The camera drops to the tabletop, follows the envelope all the way across the formica, and rises on her side.

JUNE (CONT'D)
What is that.

RAY
I wrote it when you were fifteen. Never had the guts to mail it. It's been in the glovebox of three cars.

JUNE
What does it say?

RAY
The same as what I just told you. Only worse, and with your mother's name in it.

June looks at it. She does not touch it. Then she takes it and puts it in her bag without looking.
The light through the painted harbour has gone red.

RAY (CONT'D)
There's a thing I want to ask and I know I don't have the standing to ask it.

JUNE
Then ask it badly.

RAY
Could we do this again. Not a whole thing. Not holidays. Coffee.

He puts his hand out into the middle of the table, palm up. Shaking slightly. It stays there far too long. The camera comes down to the formica and holds on that open hand, waiting the way he is waiting.
June doesn't take it. Instead she reaches out and sets the salt shaker into his palm, and closes his fingers around it.

JUNE
Not yet.

RAY
Okay.

JUNE
Ask me again in a couple weeks. I'll probably say no.

RAY
Okay.

JUNE
And then ask me the time after that.
(beat)
Not here.

RAY
Not here.

Ray nods. Once. He can't say anything else, so he doesn't try.
June looks at the door. Then she lifts the bag off her shoulder, sets it on the bench beside her, and pulls the coffee toward her with two fingers.

JUNE
It's cold.

RAY
I'll get you another one.

JUNE
Don't get up yet.

He doesn't get up.
The camera retreats. Back across the table, down over the lip of the booth, sinking to the floor, drawing away down the checkerboard aisle until the flaking harbour on the window resolves in front of them, backwards, and holds.
Two shapes in a corner booth. Not talking. Not leaving.
FADE OUT.